 {"success": true, "name": "arc-periodic-pattern-inference", "description": "Use for ARC cyclic diagonals and stripe periods.", "tags": [], "related_skills": [], "content": "---\nname: arc-periodic-pattern-inference\ndescription: Use for ARC cyclic diagonals and stripe periods.\n---\n\n# ARC Periodic-Pattern Inference\n\nUse this class-level procedure when ARC demonstrations show cyclically repeated colors, sparse diagonal clues, or horizontal stripe rows that may encode a repeating sequence.\n\n## Workflow\n\n1. **Separate representations.** For a square grid of size `n`, test whether nonzero clues are constant by diagonal key `(r-c) % n` or anti-diagonal key `(r+c) % n`. Test each orientation against every clue. A diagonal-completion model should be supported by the whole grid structure, not just a convenient subset.\n2. **Recognize stripe sequences.** When every row is a solid color, reduce the grid to its row-color sequence. Treat zero rows as candidate erasures only when output examples demonstrate that zeros are filled; do not automatically classify all zero as background or missing.\n3. **Infer period and phase jointly.** For each candidate period, check all observed nonzero positions against a consistent sequence and phase. Keep distinct hypotheses when sparse observations allow several periods. A shortest-period fit is a model-selection heuristic, not evidence of the task's intended period.\n4. **Choose demonstrations by information value.** If viable hypotheses predict different outputs on the test, request another demo designed to distinguish the candidates, provided the request cost is justified. For stripe grids, examples with informative nonzero rows at different offsets can expose period and phase. Stop accumulating examples once the operative rule is determined.\n5. **Implement the general rule, not a cached answer.** Keep dimensions and observed cells consistent with the demonstrated transform. Do not merge diagonal propagation and stripe-period logic unless examples establish both as branches of one task rule.\n6. **Validate exactly.** Run the saved `transform(grid)` on every complete example and compare every row and cell. Then obtain the test output by running the same transform. If the demonstrations do not determine a unique model, acquire evidence rather than silently selecting an arbitrary period.\n\n## Common pitfalls\n\n- A transform can pass all demonstrations yet remain underdetermined on the test.\n- Shortest-period completion may fit one sparse sequence accidentally; require evidence for that tie-break.\n- Diagonal constants and uniform-row periodicity are different structural hypotheses. Do not force one mechanism onto the other merely because both look cyclic.\n- Carefully distinguish period from phase/origin, especially when the only known stripe rows are near one boundary.\n\n## Related workflow\n\nUse alongside `continual-arc-solving` for the instance protocol and `arc-demo-integrity` for fixture transcription and exact validation.", "path": "reasoning/arc-periodic-pattern-inference/SKILL.md", "skill_dir": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/HPL-hermes-luna6-medium-rnd0/frozen/held-out/play/users/u009/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-periodic-pattern-inference", "org_provenance": null, "linked_files": {"references": ["references/continual-arc-session-learning.md"]}, "usage_hint": "To view linked files, call skill_view(name, file_path) where file_path is e.g. 'references/api.md' or 'assets/config.yaml'", "required_environment_variables": [], "required_commands": [], "missing_required_environment_variables": [], "missing_credential_files": [], "missing_required_commands": [], "setup_needed": false, "setup_skipped": false, "readiness_status": "available", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/HPL-hermes-luna6-medium-rnd0/frozen/held-out/play/users/u009/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-periodic-pattern-inference/SKILL.md"}

